## Verdict

Passed. `studio/briefs/W-130.md` revision 3 folds in the round-2 prereview's two
SHOULD, in the shapes the censor pre-endorsed. Five behaviours and every count
unchanged; one row added inside behaviour 1. No MUST was outstanding and the
round-2 prereview passed the brief, so no mechanism, interface, acceptance
criterion or scope call moves in this revision — both changes make something the
brief already authorized legible to the builder and to a reviewer reading the
run's log.

## S1 — the permitted-edits enumeration stopped undercounting

Accepted; the finding is exactly right and the contradiction was mine. Revision 2
authorized the additive `LiveOptions`/`liveFixture` extensions in *Files owned*
and then, in two other passages, called the enumerated pre-existing edits "the
only other edits" and "exactly three … anything else is a stop". A builder
reaching `liveFixture` would have halted on the brief's own instruction — the
worst kind of spec defect, because it stops correct work and looks like
discipline while doing it.

Taken in the censor's second form rather than by naming a fourth item, because
the two classes are genuinely different and counting them together is what went
wrong: **pre-existing assertion and behaviour edits are enumerated exactly, and
there are three** (`run.test.ts:670`'s `18` → `19`, the sentinel name in
`RUNNER_ENV` and `withTmp`, the `--behaviour` upper bound at `:23` with its
header comment); **behaviour-preserving additive extensions of a helper named in
Files owned are governed there**, not by that enumeration. Both passages now say
so — the *Files owned* entry and the counting rule under *Behaviours to test* —
with the in-scope test made explicit: a default-valued addition that leaves
every present `liveFixture(...)` call site answering exactly as it does today is
in scope by construction. The stop condition is restated to match: an edit that
is neither an enumerated pre-existing change nor a behaviour-preserving additive
extension of a named helper is a stop.

## S2 — the bind's selection consequence is stated, and now logged

Accepted on both counts, and the finding sharpened my own reading of the line I
added in revision 2. The `builder-run.ts` forwarding line does make the
`/browsers` bind source invoker-selectable: any absolute
`PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH` chooses which host directory becomes candidate-
readable in both cells. The brief now states that consequence where the line is
declared, in the same breath as "no cell gains a name from it", rather than
leaving the reader to infer it, and bounds it on every side it is actually
bounded on: absolute-only, `statSync`-as-directory, `realpathSync`ed before the
bind; **read-only** in both cells with `EROFS` asserted by 1(c) and 2(d) and the
host bytes re-checked unchanged after the run; nothing else about either cell
moving; and no *candidate* input able to reach the choice, since neither the
request, the red log nor the brief can set it. The honest framing, which the
brief now carries: the invoker is already the host — it supplies the repo, the
officina and the opus — so a host directory it names is not a new capability,
only a newly visible one.

Which is the second half. Revision 2 logged only the **absent** case, so the
present case — the one where a host directory becomes candidate-readable, and
where somebody chose which — was the silent branch of the pair. The runner now
prints `run-builder-host: browser cache bound read-only at /browsers from
<resolved path>` when the cache resolves, beside the existing
`no playwright browser cache found` line, and the brief says why: a read-only
bind the run does not name is a bind nobody reviewing the log can see. New row
**1(g)** asserts the line and the sentinel's realpath in it, and pairs with 1(e)
to assert that exactly one of the two lines appears in any run — never both,
never neither. The resolved path is a host path on host stderr, not into a cell
and not into a gate finding, so clause 2's token-only discipline does not reach
it; the brief notes this is the same deliberate asymmetry already granted to the
build-output dump, for the same reason.

